---
// A command example: a shell session, a config file, or a block of output.
//
//   <Terminal><pre><span class="p">$ </span>multiversectl universe ls
//   <span class="k">universe 3</span>  ·  acme-u3  ·  6 running</pre></Terminal>
//
// The <pre> belongs to the page and not to this component, which looks
// redundant and is not. Astro drops whitespace-only text nodes from a
// component's children, so a newline sitting between two elements —
// `</span>\n<span>`, which is most lines of most of these blocks — is gone
// before the component ever runs, and every command ends up on one line. The
// whitespace in a <pre> is the content; keeping the element in the page keeps
// it somewhere the compiler preserves it, and keeps that fact visible to
// whoever writes the next one.
//
// The body is written with the vocabulary the stylesheet defines. Nothing else
// in a code block is coloured:
//
//   <span class="p">$ </span>   the prompt. Coloured, and never copied: the
//                               stylesheet makes it unselectable and the copy
//                               button strips it, so what lands on the
//                               clipboard is the command without the prompt.
//   <span class="c">…</span>    a comment, muted.
//   <span class="k">…</span>    the thing to notice in the output.
//   <span class="s">…</span>    a quoted string, in a config file or a command.
//
// The Copy button is deliberately not rendered here. src/scripts/copy.ts adds
// one to every .code at runtime, so a reader with no JavaScript is never shown
// a button that cannot do anything. Keep the two in step: this component owns
// the markup, that script owns the behaviour.
---

<div class="code"><slot /></div>
